[Step 6] FULL CLOSURE attempt: elementary sign argument
==============================================================================
  g1(x) - x*(2-x-x^3) = 0  (expect 0)
  h(x):=2-x-x^3, h'(x)=-3*x**2 - 1  (negative on [0,1] since -1-3x^2<0)
  h(1) = 0  (expect 0)  => h(x)>=0 on [0,1] => g1(x)>=0
  g1''(x) = -12*x**2 - 2  (manifestly negative for all real x => g1' strictly decreasing => at most one root)
  g1'(0)=2 (>0),  g1'(1)=-4 (<0)  => exactly one root in (0,1), by IVT + strict monotonicity
  p(x)=-x^4-x, p'(x)=-4*x**3 - 1  (manifestly negative for x>=0)
  => p strictly decreasing on [0,1] => min_[0,1] p(x) = p(1) = -2

  Combining: for all n>=2, x in [0,1]:
    M2_exact/... UPPER: n*Delta_n(x) <= g1(x) <= M2_exact = 0.71072657606222206206
    LOWER: n*Delta_n(x) >= -2/(n-1)
  => |Delta_n(x)| <= max(M2_exact, 2/(n-1))/n for all n>=2, x in [0,1].

  Crossover: 2/(n-1) = M2_exact at n = [3.81402168901716]
  n_cross = 3.81402168901716  => for integer n, 2/(n-1)<=M2_exact holds iff n >= 4
  N0 = 4
    n=3: 2/(n-1) = 1 = 1.000000000   vs M2_exact = 0.7107265761   2/(n-1)<=M2_exact: False
    n=4: 2/(n-1) = 2/3 = 0.6666666667   vs M2_exact = 0.7107265761   2/(n-1)<=M2_exact: True
    n=5: 2/(n-1) = 1/2 = 0.5000000000   vs M2_exact = 0.7107265761   2/(n-1)<=M2_exact: True

  ==> THEOREM (K=2, PROVED): for all n>=4 and all x in [0,1]:
        |Delta_n(x)| = |F_n^(2)(x)-F_2(x)| <= M2_exact/n
      where M2_exact = max_x g1(x) is the EXACT real root value 0.71072657606222206206 (root of 2t^3+t-1=0, i.e. g1'(t)=0)
      This constant is SHARP: n*Delta_n(x*(n)) -> M2_exact as n->infinity (Step 4 table), so no smaller universal constant works for all large n.

  Double-check x2_star satisfies 2x^3+x-1=0: 0 (expect 0)

